# What git should ignore in a Photo Scout working directory
#
# The rule of thumb: track anything you WROTE or decided, ignore anything a
# program can rebuild. That keeps the history small and readable.

# ---- the virtual environment -------------------------------------------
# Roughly a gigabyte of installed packages, rewritten every time pip runs.
# requirements.txt is what actually records the dependencies, and that IS
# tracked, so this is all rebuildable.
Scripts/
Lib/
Include/
share/
pyvenv.cfg
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ---- generated output ---------------------------------------------------
# Thumbnails, previews, reports and the score database are all produced by a
# scoring run. Tens of thousands of files; none of it is your work.
_photo_scout/*

# ...with one deliberate exception inside that folder.
# tags.json is your tagging: hand-authored, not derived from anything, and
# not reproducible by re-running.
!_photo_scout/tags.json
#
# calibration.json is NOT excepted. It is written automatically at the end of
# a scoring run and describes one particular library, so it is worth nothing
# to anybody else and is regenerated in seconds.

# ---- databases ----------------------------------------------------------
# scores.sqlite3 is regenerated by a rescore.
# publish.sqlite3 is the Ghost upload ledger: losing it is not destructive,
# but it would re-upload every image on the next publish and leave the old
# copies orphaned in Ghost. Worth a manual backup; not worth binary churn in
# every commit.
*.sqlite3

# ---- odds and ends ------------------------------------------------------
# Historical patch scripts from development. Not part of the build.
_patch_*.py

# Stray test suites left in the repository root by an older layout. The suites
# themselves live in tests/ and ARE tracked; the leading slash anchors this to
# the root so it cannot match tests/_selftest_*.py.
/_selftest*.py

# HANDOFF.md is a working note for a specific machine and person, not part of
# the project. Kept local.
HANDOFF.md

# The PowerShell helpers: one-time git setup and a save-and-push wrapper.
# Local conveniences, kept out of the history by request. NOTE: neither is
# restored by a fresh clone, so keep a copy if you rebuild this folder from
# GitHub.
git-setup.ps1
save.ps1

# Credentials, and the helper that asks for them.
#
# keys.secret.xml is the one that MUST never be committed. It holds the Ghost
# Admin API key and the heart admin token, encrypted with Windows DPAPI under
# one account on one machine - useless to anybody else, but still a credential
# store and still nobody's business but yours. The whole *.secret.* pattern is
# ignored so a second one cannot be created with a name this list has not
# anticipated.
#
# set-keys.ps1 contains no secrets at all - it only prompts - so it would be
# safe to track. It is ignored because it is a local convenience like the two
# above, and like them it is NOT restored by a fresh clone.
set-keys.ps1
keys.secret.xml
*.secret.*

psc-web-tags.json
*.tmp
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
